    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i'm working on a low-fat mayo formulation and having issues with phase separation in the high-acid stages. can you explain the specific molecular role of lecithin in stabilizing the oil-water interface when the pH is low?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin, a powerful emulsifier, acts as a molecular bridge between the oil droplets and the surrounding aqueous phase, especially when the pH is low. In the high-acid environment, the charged groups on the lecithin molecules become protonated, which can sometimes affect their surface activity, but they still function by positioning themselves at the oil-water interface. These molecules have hydrophilic heads and lipophilic tails, which arrange themselves so the heads face the water and the tails embed in the oil, creating a stable barrier that prevents the oil droplets from coalescing and separating. This stabilization is crucial because it keeps the dispersed oil droplets suspended uniformly in the acidic matrix.",
