    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. if the electrostatic repulsion is what's failing, how much of a role does the kinetic energy of the system play here? i'm worried that the droplets are acting a bit too \"unruly\" during the high-shear mixing phase, almost like they're actively trying to avoid the lecithin coating or just chaotic in their movement. is there a way to predict this kind of unpredictable aggregation behavior?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The kinetic energy of the system, driven by high-shear mixing, is the very force that tries to overcome the stabilizing forces you've built up. If the kinetic energy—the sheer chaotic motion—is too high, it can force droplets into close contact faster than the lecithin can re-establish a strong repulsive barrier, leading to aggregation. It's like trying to keep a swarm of tiny, grumpy, and highly energetic things from bumping into each other; if the energy is too high, they'll just crash into each other regardless of the small repulsive force. Predicting this chaotic behavior is difficult, but you can model it using concepts like the DLVO theory, which balances attractive van der Waals forces against the repulsive electrostatic forces. If the energy input (shear) is constantly pushing the droplets past the energy barrier provided by the lecithin, they will clump.",
      "source": "target",
